I see it now. Line 247-249:

```js
patchRuntimeExec() {
    node_red_runtime_exec_1.default.init(node_red_1.default.runtime._, this.status);
}
```

And line 250-252:
```js
get exec() {
    return node_red_runtime_exec_1.default;
}
```

And `execNpmInstall` calls `this.exec.run(exports.NPM_COMMAND, ...)` where `this.exec` returns our custom exec.

Now, let me see WHERE `patchRuntimeExec()` is called. This is crucial. If it's called AFTER `startRED()` (line 253), then the installer might have already captured the original exec before we patch it.

Wait, but the `init()` function replaces `_runtime.exec` with `this` (our patched exec). Since the installer accesses `runtime.exec.run()` at call time (not cached), even if patchRuntimeExec is called after startRED, the installer would still use our patched exec... unless the installer caches the reference.

But actually, looking at the NR5 installer code:
```js
return exec.run(process.execPath, args, { cwd: installDir }, true)
```

The `exec` here is the one passed to the install function, which ultimately comes from `runtime.exec` (set up in `runtime/index.js` as `exec: exec` where `exec` is `require("@node-red/util").exec`).

When we call `init(node_red_1.default.runtime._, this.status)`, we set `_runtime.exec = this`. So after init, `runtime._.exec` (and thus `runtime.exec`) is our patched exec.

But if `patchRuntimeExec()` is called AFTER `RED.start()` (which starts the runtime, including loading flows and the installer), then the installer methods might have already been called before our patch takes effect.

Let me check where `patchRuntimeExec()` is called in the startup sequence.

